                  <accessrestrict>
                     <p>The original letter is unlocated, a copy, transcription, or published version survives</p>
                  </accessrestrict>
                  <altformavail>
                     <p>a copy of the letter in the hand of William Cole, annotated in his hand "This I had by Letter from my Friend <emph render="underline">M<emph render="super">r.</emph> Gray of Pembroke Hall in Cambridge</emph>, to whom I sent for the Receit, having seen &amp; smelt the odoriferous Jar in his Chambers the year before.", is at Add. MSS 5825, ff. 283<emph render="super">b</emph>, 284<emph render="super">b</emph>, Manuscripts collection, British Library, London, UK</p>
                  </altformavail>
                  <bibliography>
                     <bibref title="T/W_1971">
                        <emph render="italic">Correspondence</emph> (1971), letter no. 388**, vol. iii, 1322-1323</bibref>
                  </bibliography>
                  <scopecontent>
                     <p>Incipit: "Get some coarse brown Bay Salt: this is the sine quâ non, &amp; (by the way)". Sent from <geogname normal="Cambridge (Cambridgeshire, England, United Kingdom), inhabited place [7010874]"
                                  source="tgn">Cambridge</geogname>. Dateline: "July 7. 1764". dateline in the closer. Address: "Cambr". address in the closer.</p>
                  </scopecontent>
